{
  "raw": {
    "https://albany.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205818": {
      "title": "Senior Staff Assistant - Office of Provost & VP - Academic Affairs",
      "description": "University at Albany\nJob Posting: Senior Staff Assistant - Office of Provost & VP - Academic Affairs University at Albany \n\tJob Posting: Senior Staff Assistant - Office of Provost & VP - Academic Affairs Title Home About Employment Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Senior Staff Assistant - Office of Provost & VP - Academic Affairs Category: Promotional Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Provost & VP- Academic Affairs - 02001 Locations: Albany, NY Posted: Oct 15, 2026 Closes: Oct 28, 2026 - 11:59 PM EDT Type: Full-time Ref. No.: WF260161p Position ID: 205818 Share About University at Albany: The University at Albany is a Carnegie-classified Research 1 institution driven by academic excellence, pioneering research and scientific discovery, and service to community. UAlbany's world-class faculty experts and approximately 17,500 students are creating new knowledge in fields such as artificial intelligence, semiconductor engineering, atmospheric and environmental sciences, cybersecurity, public health, public administration and social welfare while deepening our understanding of our world through scholarship and creative activity in the social sciences and humanities. As one of the most diverse public research institutions in the nation, the University is a national leader in public engagement, educational equity and social mobility. For over 180 years, UAlbany has molded bright, curious and engaged students and launched them toward success. Located in Albany, New York, New York State's capital, the University is convenient to Boston, New York City, and the Adirondacks. Job Description: In the Office of the Provost and Senior Associate Vice President for Academic Affairs, the Senior Staff Assistant provides strategic and operational support for key functions and services. This role works closely with the Associate Vice Provost for Faculty Development and serves as a liaison across a range of offices and initiatives. This position also supports strategic initiatives within the Provost's Office by coordinating implementation, tracking progress, and ensuring the timely advancement of key priorities. Reporting to the Senior Associate Vice President/Chief of Staff, the Faculty Development Specialist collaborates regularly with the Associate Vice Provosts and the Assistant Vice President for Administration. The incumbent will routinely interact with members of the University Senate who are on the Council for Promotion and Continuing Appointment, Deans Leadership Team and Department Chairs. Primary Responsibilities: Faculty Development Support: Coordinate faculty personnel processes for tenure-track faculty, including tenure, promotion, and term renewals, in collaboration with the Office of Human Resources and academic units. Support the Council on Promotion and Continuing Appointment (CPCA) by coordinating meetings, preparing materials, documenting outcomes and drafting summary reports, following up with Deans staff and Department Chairs on any concerns. Administer faculty-related programs and initiatives, including Joint Labor Management programs (e.g., Drescher). Oversee data collection, management, and tracking of faculty personnel processes, and provide reports as needed. Maintain secure, auditable records of faculty promotions and contract renewals. General Operational Support: Support the daily operations of the Provost's Office, including managing operational procedures, communications and routing inquiries. Serve as a liaison with campus partners, facilitating communication and collaboration across Academic Affairs and administrative units. Contribute to institutional initiatives, including reporting, committee support, and special projects. Identify and implement opportunities to streamline workflows and enhance efficiency within faculty support processes. Facilitate clear communication with faculty, department chairs, and university offices about policies, procedures, and deadlines. Review and process voluntary appointments. Provide administrative support for Provost office procedures related to faculty searches. Oversee the Work-Study Process for the Provosts Office Serve as the liaison with the Office of Financial Aid Work-Study unit. Create and post adds and hire Work-Study Students. Supervise Work-Study students. Oversee the Provost Office Self-Study Process Update SMART goal progress annually. Draft the Self-Study and Self-Study report updates. Coordinate data collection and reporting related to strategic priorities, including annual SMART goal tracking and self-study updates. Office Operations: Represent Academic Affairs on the University Incident Management Team (IMT) and support emergency preparedness, response efforts, and continuity planning. Update the Provost Office Continuity Plan/Documentation annually. Coordinate annual updates to the electronic Faculty Handbook. Develop and maintain the Provost Office Standard Operating Procedures Manual. Serve as the AA Sustainability Committee liaison with the Sustainability Office. Other Reasonable Duties as Assigned Functional and Supervisory Relationships: Reports to: Senior Associate Vice President and Chief of Staff for Academic Affairs Supervises the following positions: Work-Study Student(s) Job Requirements: Strong oral, written, and organizational skills with the ability to manage multiple priorities and maintain accurate records. Proficiency in Microsoft Outlook, Word, and Excel. Ability to handle confidential, personal, and sensitive information with a high degree of discretion, professionalism, and adherence to privacy requirements. A range of skills to build trust with staff, faculty and administrators, and communicate effectively and confidentially with a range of stakeholders. Capacity to bring rigor and care to complex, high-stakes processes. Ability to serve as a trusted resource for faculty concerns, conflict resolution, and academic personnel matters, fostering a respectful and professional culture. Applicants must demonstrate an ability to develop inclusive and equitable relationships within our diverse campus community. Applicants must demonstrate an ability to support diversity, equity, access, inclusion, and belonging relative to their role. Requirements: Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor's degree from a college or University accredited by the US Department of Education or an internationally recognized accrediting organization. 3 to 4 years of relevant professional experience such as project management, handling confidential personnel matters, developing and updating procedures, working with multiple constituencies. Preferred Qualifications: Master's degree from a college or University accredited by the US Department of Education or an internationally recognized accrediting organization. Professional experience working within higher education. Experience with PeopleSoft. Experience with Business Intelligence (BI) software. Working Environment: Typical office environment Additional Information: This is a promotional opportunity for current UAlbany employees. Eligibility for Consideration: You must be employed at the University at Albany campus. You must be in a State-funded UUP professional position (MC employees are not eligible). You must have a permanent, term, or probationary appointment. Only temporary employees employed by UAlbany for three or more consecutive years can be considered eligible. For details concerning the University's Promotion Policy for Professional Employees, please see HR Memorandum 88-4 . Professional Rank and Salary Grade: Senior Staff Assistant, SL3, $68,000-72,000. Special Note: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. If you currently need sponsorship or will need it in the future to maintain employment authorization, you do not meet eligibility requirements. Additionally, please note that UAlbany is not an E-Verify employer. The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, or Clery Act, mandates that all Title IV institutions, without exception, prepare, publish and distribute an Annual Security Report. This report consists of two basic parts: disclosure of the University's crime statistics for the past three years; and disclosures regarding the University's current campus security policies. The University at Albany's Annual Security Report is available in portable document format [PDF] by clicking this link http://police.albany.edu/ASR.shtml Pursuant to New York Labor Law §194-a, the University will not seek, request, or rely upon an applicant's wage or salary history in determining whether to interview, hire, or establish compensation for an applicant. Applicants are not required to provide wage or salary history information as a condition of employment consideration. Salary history may only be verified in the limited circumstances permitted by law. If you have a disability and require a reasonable accommodation to participate in the application process, including interviews, please contact Rhonda Haines, the University's Designee for Reasonable Accommodations (DRA) in the Office of Human Resources, at rlhaines@albany.edu or (518) 437-4700; requests for accommodation will be considered in accordance with applicable law. THE UNIVERSITY AT ALBANY IS AN EO/AA/IRCA/ADA EMPLOYER Please apply online via http://albany.interviewexchange.com/candapply.jsp?JOBID=205818 Application Instructions: Applicants MUST submit the following documents: Resume Cover letter stating all the required minimum qualifications and any of the applicable preferred qualifications List of 3 professional references with e-mail addresses and telephone numbers See the FAQ for using our online system. Please contact us if you need assistance applying through this website. Returning Applicants - Login to your UAlbany Careers Account to check your completed application. Closing date for receipt of applications: October 28. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University at Albany, State University of New York 1400 Washington Ave Albany, NY 12222"
    },
    "https://albany.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205754": {
      "title": "Assistant Provost for Library and Information Services - Office of Library and Information Services",
      "description": "University at Albany\nJob Posting: Assistant Provost for Library and Information Services - Office of Library and Information Services University at Albany \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Provost for Library and Information Services - Office of Library and Information Services Title Home About Employment Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Provost for Library and Information Services - Office of Library and Information Services Category: Professional Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Office of Library and Information Services - 03094 Locations: Albany, NY Posted: Oct 14, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: WF260079 Position ID: 205754 Share About University at Albany: The University at Albany is a Carnegie-classified Research 1 institution driven by academic excellence, pioneering research and scientific discovery, and service to community. UAlbany's world-class faculty experts and approximately 17,500 students are creating new knowledge in fields such as artificial intelligence, semiconductor engineering, atmospheric and environmental sciences, cybersecurity, public health, public administration and social welfare while deepening our understanding of our world through scholarship and creative activity in the social sciences and humanities. As one of the most diverse public research institutions in the nation, the University is a national leader in public engagement, educational equity and social mobility. For over 180 years, UAlbany has molded bright, curious and engaged students and launched them toward success. Located in Albany, New York, New York State's capital, the University is convenient to Boston, New York City, and the Adirondacks. Job Description: Digital Innovation and Academic Services (DIAS) provides system-wide academic and digital services that support teaching, learning, research, student success, and operational excellence across the State University of New York. The Office of Library and Information Services (OLIS) is responsible for advancing library and information services through shared programs, technologies, collections, resource sharing initiatives, licensing agreements, and strategic partnerships that support SUNY's 64 campuses. OLIS works collaboratively with campus libraries, academic leaders, researchers, and external partners to strengthen access to information resources, support scholarly communication and research activities, and promote innovation in library services and emerging technologies. This position provides system-level leadership for library and information services across the State University of New York, guiding strategy, operations, and shared services that support SUNY's 64 campuses. The Assistant Provost leads the strategic use of emerging technologies, including artificial intelligence (AI), and supports the digital transformation of information ecosystems for teaching, learning, research, and library services. As the primary system voice for shared library initiatives, the Assistant Provost connects technology, scholarship, teaching, research, and resource sharing to strengthen SUNY's knowledge infrastructure and maximize impact across the system. This position serves as a system-wide policy leader, contributing to the development, interpretation, implementation, and evaluation of policies, procedures, and standards that guide library and information services across SUNY. Primary Responsibilities: Provide strategic leadership for the planning, development, delivery, communication, and promotion of system-wide library and information services, ensuring alignment with SUNY priorities. Develop, implement, interpret, and oversee system-wide policies, standards, and guidelines related to library services, information resources, digital platforms, emerging technologies, and information access to support compliance, consistency, and operational effectiveness across SUNY. Direct the operations of the Office of Library and Information Services, including oversight of budgets, staffing, organizational planning, technology investments, and resource allocation. Provide oversight for shared licensing programs, contract negotiations, vendor contracts, assessments, analytics, reporting activities, and evidence-based decision-making processes that support continuous improvement and strategic planning. Lead system-wide initiatives related to shared library services, scholarly communication, repository services, research support, research data management, digital scholarship, open science, emerging technologies, artificial intelligence, and equitable information access. Lead the strategy, implementation, administration, and continuous improvement of shared library systems, technologies, and digital infrastructure that support teaching, learning, research, resource sharing, and scholarly communication across SUNY. Develop and maintain collaborative relationships with SUNY campuses, external organizations, consortia, vendors, professional associations, strategic partners, and the Center for Professional Development to advance shared services, workforce development, and resource-sharing initiatives. Supervise, mentor, and develop OLIS staff while fostering a collaborative, inclusive, and high-performing organizational culture. Functional and Supervisory Relationships: Reports to: Senior Associate Provost for Digital Innovation and Academic Services Collaborates with: Campus library directors and staff, academic leadership, research administration, information technology leadership, SUNY System Administration offices, external consortia, vendors, service providers, and professional organizations. Supervises the following positions: Director of Shared Library Services Associate Director for OLIS Licensing Associate Director for Assessment and Data Analytics Administrative Assistant for OLIS Job Requirements: Strategic leadership and organizational planning skills. Knowledge of library operations, information services, scholarly communication, and higher education environments. Ability to develop and manage complex partnerships involving campuses, vendors, consortia, and external organizations. Knowledge of emerging technologies, digital transformation initiatives, artificial intelligence, and evolving information ecosystems. Strong financial management, budgeting, procurement, and contract administration skills. Experience utilizing data, analytics, and assessment to support decision-making and organizational improvement. Ability to lead organizational change and manage complex system-wide initiatives. Strong written, verbal, presentation, and interpersonal communication skills. Ability to supervise, mentor, and develop professional staff. Strong relationship-building, consensus-development, and stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to establish and maintain collaborative partnerships across diverse constituencies and complex organizational environments. Commitment to fostering inclusive, accessible, and equitable services and workplace practices. Applicants must demonstrate an ability to develop inclusive and equitable relationships within our diverse campus community. Applicants must demonstrate an ability to support diversity, equity, access, inclusion, and belonging relative to their role. Requirements: Minimum Qualifications: Master's degree or higher from a college or university accredited by the U.S. Department of Education or an internationally recognized accrediting organization. At least six years of progressively responsible leadership experience in higher education libraries, including library information systems, research support, resource sharing, and other technology-enabled services. Experience managing personnel, budgets, and organizational resources. Demonstrated experience in strategic planning and leading strategy execution, operations, programs, or services within a complex organization. Experience overseeing technology-enabled services, digital platforms, or enterprise systems that support teaching, learning, research, or operational objectives. Preferred Qualifications: Experience operating effectively within large, multi-institutional or public higher education environments, navigating system-level priorities alongside campus needs. Experience leading large-scale, cross-institution initiatives, including setting direction, aligning stakeholders, and delivering results in complex environments. Demonstrated capacity to manage complex partnerships, including vendor relationships, procurement processes, and external collaborations across diverse stakeholders. Familiarity with statewide or large-scale procurement and contracting environments, with the ability to navigate varied regulatory, legal, and institutional frameworks. Demonstrated ability to shape and guide collaborative strategies for resource access, including shared investments, partnerships, and coordinated service models. Experience with multiple approaches to expanding access to scholarly resources, such as collective licensing, shared collections, and resource sharing networks, and the ability to make strategic decisions across these models. Experience managing shared services, licensing programs, vendor relationships, or collaborative resource initiatives. Demonstrated capacity to lead and manage distributed or remote teams, with an understanding of the intentional practices required to support fully remote collaboration, communication, and organizational culture. Project and program management experience, including prioritization, coordination, and delivery across complex and distributed environments. Working Environment: Typical office environment with remote work flexibility consistent with University policy. Occasional travel required to SUNY campuses, conferences, professional meetings, and external partner organizations. Position requires sustained interaction with campus leaders, library professionals, vendors, and external stakeholders. Work involves strategic planning, complex problem-solving, budget oversight, personnel management, and oversight of multiple concurrent initiatives and priorities. Additional Information: Professional Rank and Salary Range: Assistant Provost, MP, $120,000-130,000. Special Notes: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. If you currently need sponsorship or will need it in the future to maintain employment authorization, you do not meet eligibility requirements. Additionally, please note that UAlbany is not an E-Verify employer. The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, or Clery Act, mandates that all Title IV institutions, without exception, prepare, publish, and distribute an Annual Security Report. This report consists of two basic parts: disclosure of the University's crime statistics for the past three years; and disclosures regarding the University's current campus security policies. The University at Albany's Annual Security Report is available in portable document format [PDF] by clicking this link http://police.albany.edu/ASR.shtml Pursuant to New York Labor Law §194-a, the University will not seek, request, or rely upon an applicant's wage or salary history in determining whether to interview, hire, or establish compensation for an applicant. Applicants are not required to provide wage or salary history information as a condition of employment consideration. Salary history may only be verified in the limited circumstances permitted by law. If you have a disability and require a reasonable accommodation to participate in the application process, including interviews, please contact Rhonda Haines, the University's Designee for Reasonable Accommodations (DRA) in the Office of Human Resources, at rlhaines@albany.edu or (518) 437-4700; requests for accommodation will be considered in accordance with applicable law. THE UNIVERSITY AT ALBANY IS AN EO/AA/IRCA/ADA EMPLOYER Please apply online via http://albany.interviewexchange.com/candapply.jsp?JOBID=205754 Application Instructions: Applicants MUST submit the following documents: Resume/CV Cover letter stating all the required minimum qualifications and any of the applicable preferred qualifications Contact information for three professional references Note: After submitting your resume/CV, the subsequent pages give you instructions for uploading additional documents (i.e. cover letter etc.). See the FAQ for using our online system. Please contact us if you need assistance applying through this website. Returning Applicants - Login to your UAlbany Careers Account to check your completed application. Preference will be given to applications received by October 27. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University at Albany, State University of New York 1400 Washington Ave Albany, NY 12222"
    },
    "https://albany.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205741": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant 1;Trainee 1 & 2 (NY HELPS) (Educational & Counseling Psychology)",
      "description": "University at Albany\nJob Posting: Administrative Assistant 1;Trainee 1 & 2 (NY HELPS) (Educational & Counseling Psychology) University at Albany \n\tJob Posting: Administrative Assistant 1;Trainee 1 & 2 (NY HELPS) (Educational & Counseling Psychology) Title Home About Employment Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Administrative Assistant 1;Trainee 1 & 2 (NY HELPS) (Educational & Counseling Psychology) Category: Civil Service Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Educational & Counseling Psychology - 02305 Locations: Albany, NY Posted: Oct 13, 2026 Closes: Oct 24, 2026 - 11:59 PM EDT Type: Full-time Ref. No.: WF260126, Line 01483 Salary Type: Annually Position ID: 205741 Share About University at Albany: The University at Albany is a Carnegie-classified Research 1 institution driven by academic excellence, pioneering research and scientific discovery, and service to community.  UAlbany's world-class faculty experts and approximately 17,500 students are creating new knowledge in fields such as artificial intelligence, semiconductor engineering, atmospheric and environmental sciences, cybersecurity, public health, public administration and social welfare while deepening our understanding of our world through scholarship and creative activity in the social sciences and humanities.  As one of the most diverse public research institutions in the nation, the University is a national leader in public engagement, educational equity and social mobility.  For over 180 years, UAlbany has molded bright, curious and engaged students and launched them toward success. Located in Albany, New York, New York State's capital, the University is convenient to Boston, New York City, and the Adirondacks Job Description: Vacancy #: WF260126 Apply by: October 24, 2026 Title: Administrative Assistant 1; Trainee 1 & 2 (NY HELPS) Location: Educational & Counseling Psychology Salary: $49,841 Grade: CSEA SG-11 Hours: Academic Year 8:30am-4:30pm (Summers 8:00am-4:00pm) ½ hour lunch AY; ½ hour lunch Summer Duration: Contingent Permanent Requirements: This title is part of the New York Hiring for Emergency Limited Placement Statewide Program (NY HELPS). For the duration of the NY HELPS Program, this title maybe filled via a non-competitive appointment, which means no examination is required but all candidates must meet the minimum qualifications of the title for which they apply. At a future date (within one year of permanent appointment), it is expected employees hired under NY HELPS will have their non-competitive employment status converted to competitive status, without having to compete in an examination. Employees will then be afforded with all of the same rights and privileges of competitive class employees of New York State. While serving permanently in a NY HELPS title, employees may take part in any promotion examination for which they are qualified. NON-COMPETITIVE MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (NY HELPS): Administrative Assistant Trainee 1 (Non-Statutory Equated Salary Grade 8) 2 YEAR Traineeship / coursework requirements: Six months of experience in administrative support, which includes use of office software (e.g., email, word processing), provision of customer service, business writing, and/or office administration. Administrative Assistant Trainee 2 (Equated Grade 10) 1 YEAR Traineeship / coursework requirements: One year of experience in administrative support, which includes use of office software (e.g., email, word processing), provision of customer service, business writing, and/or office administration. Administrative Assistant 1, (Salary Grade 11) NO Traineeship / no coursework requirements: Two years of experience in administrative support, which includes use of office software (e.g., email, word processing), provision of customer service, business writing, and/or office administration. Substitution: Certification (e.g., IAAP Certified Administrative Professional) or associate's degree* in office administration, secretarial science, administrative assistance, paralegal, business technology, or office technology substitutes for two years of experience. *Your degree must have been awarded by a college or university accredited by a regional, national, or specialized agency recognized as an accrediting agency by the U.S. Department of Education/U.S. Secretary of Education. If your degree was awarded by an educational institution outside the United States or its territories, you must provide independent verification of equivalency. This information can be found at http://www.cs.ny.gov/jobseeker/degrees.cfm. Candidates are responsible for the evaluation fee. COMPETITIVE MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (NON-NY HELPS): Administrative Assistant 1, SG-11: Successful completion of 52 weeks of service as an Administrative Assistant Trainee 2 and completion of the four core administrative support courses AND completion of the four elective administrative support courses, as designated by the agency   OR Reachable on an appropriate eligible list and / or eligible to transfer and successful completion of the four core administrative support courses AND completion of four elective administrative support courses, as designated by the agency. OR Have a current permanent appointment with 1 year of service as an Administrative Assistant 1 OR Be a former state employee eligible for reinstatement consideration (previous permanent service in the Secretary 1 title eligible for reinstatement to the title of Administrative Assistant 1; previous permanent service in other qualifying titles requires completion of core and / or elective administrative competency coursework prior to appointment above Trainee level 1). Administrative Assistant Trainee Level 1, Equated Salary Grade 8:  2 Year Traineeship leading to SG-11 Reachable on an appropriate eligible list and / or be a current state employee eligible to transfer to the grade 8 traineeship. Administrative Assistant Trainee Level 2, Equated Salary Grade 10:  1 Year Traineeship leading to SG-11 Successful completion of 52 weeks of service as an Administrative Assistant Trainee 1 AND completion of the four core administrative support courses; OR Reachable on an appropriate eligible list or eligible to transfer AND completion of the four core administrative support courses. May be filled from a mandatory reemployment list if one is in effect at the time of appointment. Any resumes received that do not meet the above requirements as described will be deemed unqualified. Additional Information: Special Notes: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. If you currently need sponsorship or will need it in the future to maintain employment authorization, you do not meet eligibility requirements. Additionally, please note that UAlbany is not an E-Verify employer. The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, or Clery Act, mandates that all Title IV institutions, without exception, prepare, publish, and distribute an Annual Security Report. This report consists of two basic parts: disclosure of the University's crime statistics for the past three years; and disclosures regarding the University's current campus security policies. The University at Albany's Annual Security Report is available in portable document format [PDF] by clicking this link http://police.albany.edu/ASR.shtml Pursuant to New York Labor Law §194-a, the University will not seek, request, or rely upon an applicant's wage or salary history in determining whether to interview, hire, or establish compensation for an applicant. Applicants are not required to provide wage or salary history information as a condition of employment consideration. Salary history may only be verified in the limited circumstances permitted by law. If you have a disability and require a reasonable accommodation to participate in the application process, including interviews, please contact Rhonda Haines, the University's Designee for Reasonable Accommodations (DRA) in the Office of Human Resources, at rlhaines@albany.edu or (518) 437-4700; requests for accommodation will be considered in accordance with applicable law. THE UNIVERSITY AT ALBANY IS AN EO/AA/IRCA/ADA EMPLOYER Application Instructions: Applicants must submit the below documents through the online application process.  Accommodations can be provided to applicants who need assistance applying online. Classified Employment Application Form Employment and Experience Form Optional: Resume and/or cover letter may be uploaded as an additional document. Please note that the application and employment forms must still be completed in their entirety. Note : The required forms are included in the online application process, which will be found after clicking \"apply now\" See the FAQ for using our online system. Please contact us if you need assistance applying through this website. Returning Applicants - Login to your UAlbany Careers Account to check your completed application. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University at Albany, State University of New York 1400 Washington Ave Albany, NY 12222"
